What do I know about MDGEN from general knowledge? MDGEN is a diffusion-based generative model for molecular dynamics trajectories (from the paper "MDGEN: a diffusion model for sampling molecular dynamics trajectories" — actually I recall "MDGEN: A Diffusion Model for Sampling Molecular Dynamics" from 2025, by a group... Let me recall. There's a paper "Generative hierarchical diffusion for the reconstruction and generation of molecular dynamics trajectories" — MDGEN. Published on arXiv in 2025. It uses a latent diffusion model conditioned on a fixed number of frames (subsampled trajectory) to generate full-resolution trajectories. Actually, MDGEN generates MD trajectories at full temporal resolution from a coarse-grained/subsampled input. It was trained on MD17, MD22, and a protein (chignolin) datasets. It operates on Cartesian coordinates (all-atom), conditioned on a subsampled sequence of frames, generating the high-resolution trajectory autoregressively or via diffusion in blocks.

Let me recall more precisely. The MDGEN paper: "MDGEN: A Diffusion Model for Sampling Molecular Dynamics Trajectories" — arXiv 2503.10612 (2025). They propose generating full-resolution trajectories from subsampled trajectories. The model conditions on sparse frames (e.g., every Nth frame of an MD trajectory at 0.5 fs in MD17 / MD22, subsampled by a factor, e.g., keep 1/10) and outputs the dense trajectory. It's a latent diffusion model with a temporal encoder-decoder (VQ-VAE-like?) Actually I think they use an architecture with time embedding.

